     "result_text_tail": "% growth, and the recommended rise covers 33%. New business and SMB retention have to carry the rest.\n- **Customer Success's claim:** \"halve SMB churn\" would mean going below the pre-promotion rate. Fixing the promotion cohort fully would cut SMB churn by about a third.\n- **Board dashboard:** its \"1.4% \u2192 2.3%\" churn figure doesn't match the segment table under any weighting I tried, so I left it out. The next steps include replacing it with a defined measure.\n\n**Sources:** all 101 numbers trace to the briefing pack or to calculations from it, and the workbook holds live formulas for the calculations. No \"?\" placeholders remain. Two QA warnings are left, both because the pack states the figure in words (\"three times\", \"halve\"); the excerpts are correct.\n\n**Choices I made without you**\n- I approved the storyline as drafted and used the navy theme, with the CEO and leadership team as the audience.\n- I set the mid-market rise at 10% because that's the tested level; 12% would add about $0.7M but is untested. The SMB test uses 8%, the 2023 level.\n- I assumed the 7% caps cover 60% of the enterprise ARR renewing in 2027, the same share as the whole enterprise book. The pack doesn't say.\n- All dollar figures are before any churn the price rise itself causes.\n\nI can switch the theme, test a 12% mid-market option, or add a net-of-churn sensitivity if you want one.\n\nFiles are in `deliverable/`:\n- palisade-pricing-2027.pptx\n- palisade-pricing-2027-sources.xlsx\n- palisade-pricing-2027.json"
    }
